
Template: piwik/automatic-upgrade
Type: boolean
Default: false
_Description: Run Matomo's database migrations automatically?
 This question is never put to you. The template exists so that an answer
 given to the older piwik package can be read and carried over to
 matomo/automatic-upgrade, which is the question actually asked.

Template: matomo/automatic-upgrade
Type: boolean
Default: false
_Description: Run Matomo's database migrations automatically?
 Matomo's database schema has to be migrated from time to time, so that
 Matomo keeps working once its code has been upgraded. This package can run
 that migration for you, as part of the package upgrade that makes it
 necessary.
 .
 Whichever you choose, take a database backup first and keep taking them: a
 major version brings many migrations, and on a large database they can run
 for a long time.
 .
 If enabled, the migration runs during this upgrade, and during every later
 package upgrade that needs one.
 .
 If disabled, the migration is left to you after each package upgrade.
 Matomo shows the queries it needs run in its own interface, and this
 package prints the command that runs them.
 .
 You can change your answer later with: dpkg-reconfigure matomo-vanilla

Template: matomo/upgrade_warning
Type: boolean
Default: false
_Description: Continue with this major version upgrade?
 The package installed here is older than Matomo 5, which its developers
 treat as a major version: the upgrade brings a large number of database
 migrations.
 .
 If you do not continue, the installation stops here. Nothing on this system
 is changed, and the upgrade will still be waiting whenever you come back to
 it.
 .
 Before continuing, take a database backup and make sure you know how to
 restore it. The migrations are run by PHP, with this command, and you may
 have to look into a failure and run it again:
 .
 php /usr/share/matomo/console core:update

Template: matomo-vanilla/archiving-keep-cron
Type: boolean
Default: true
_Description: Keep archiving reports from your existing cron job?
 Report archiving is running on this system from /etc/cron.d/matomo-archive,
 a file this package no longer ships: archiving is a systemd timer now,
 matomo.timer.
 .
 Keeping the cron job changes nothing about how archiving runs today. The
 file is put back where it is, with your schedule and your options
 untouched, and the timer is installed but left inactive. You can switch
 over whenever you like with: systemctl enable --now matomo.timer
 .
 Switching to the timer hands the schedule to systemd, which also records
 each run in the journal. If your cron line carried options -- an explicit
 --url, typically -- carry them over yourself: your old file is kept beside
 the new one as /etc/cron.d/matomo-archive.dpkg-bak, and options belong in a
 drop-in rather than in the unit.

Template: matomo-vanilla/archiving-enable-timer
Type: boolean
Default: true
_Description: Enable automatic report archiving?
 Matomo computes its reports in the background, and will not show much until
 that runs. This package provides it as a systemd timer, matomo.timer, which
 runs hourly.
 .
 Enabling it here is safe on a fresh installation: until Matomo has been set
 up through its web installer, each run is skipped rather than failed, and
 archiving begins on its own once the setup is finished.
 .
 If you would rather drive archiving yourself -- from your own cron job, or
 not at all -- decline, and nothing is enabled. The timer stays available:
 systemctl enable --now matomo.timer

Template: matomo-vanilla/archiving-decision
Type: string
Default: none
Description: for internal use; which archiving question applied
 Not a question: the config script records here which of the archiving questions
 the state of this system called for, and the postinst reads it to know which
 answer to act on.
 .
 It cannot be worked out from the answers themselves. Preseeding marks a question
 as seen, which is its whole purpose, so "has this been seen" says nothing about
 which question was actually put -- a preseeded answer to a question that did not
 apply would otherwise be acted upon.


Template: matomo-vanilla/webserver-alias
Type: boolean
Default: false
_Description: Serve Matomo at /matomo on every site of this web server?
 The package gives the web server access to /usr/share/matomo but does not
 publish it by itself. Accepting here adds an alias so that http://<any site on
 this server>/matomo/ answers immediately -- Apache through the conf-enabled
 snippet matomo-alias.conf, lighttpd through its conf-enabled/50-matomo.conf.
 .
 Consider what that opens. Until you have completed Matomo's web installer,
 anyone who can reach this server can open that address and run the installer
 themselves, including creating the administrator account. On a server
 reachable from the Internet, decline, and either complete the installer
 right after this, or give Matomo a virtual host of its own.
 .
 Declining changes nothing. The alias can be enabled later with
 "a2enconf matomo-alias" (Apache) or "lighty-enable-mod matomo" (lighttpd),
 or by answering again: dpkg-reconfigure matomo-vanilla.
